Close the five holes a review found, and prove each one closed with a test

A reviewer went at the app with the brief in one hand and curl in the other, and
reproduced every critical finding with real output rather than describing it. All five
are now fixed, and each has a test that fails if the fix is ever removed.

ANYONE COULD CONFIRM ANYTHING. There was no session, no CSRF token and no roles: a
cross-origin POST from evil.example wrote a confirmation and queued a task with an
attacker-chosen title and owner. There is now a signed, HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict
session cookie, a CSRF token bound to that session so a third party cannot mint one, a
sign-in page, and a role check - a viewer may look and may not act, and the refusal is
audited. It is still password-less, which is stated plainly on the sign-in page and in
the README, but a confirmation is now attributable to whoever held the session, which
is what ADR 0002 actually asks for.

TWO WORKERS WROTE THE SAME TASK TWICE. Claiming a job was a read, then a check, then an
update, so two workers saw the same queued row: 150 confirmations produced 158 tracker
writes. The claim is now a single UPDATE ... RETURNING that cannot be interleaved, and
the tracker write is conditional on the state it read, so a loser updates zero rows and
writes, meters and audits nothing.

THE SIGNING SECRET SHIPPED IN THE SOURCE, and an unknown team_id created its own tenant
on the way past, so a forged event conjured a workspace and injected drafts into it.
The secret now has no default and the process refuses to boot without one, and the
webhook opens a workspace rather than ensuring it: an event for a tenant we do not have
is a 404, not an invitation.

THE SIGNED BYTES WERE NOT THE PARSED BYTES. The raw body was only captured for JSON, so
a urlencoded request verified an HMAC over the empty string and then handed the handler
content nobody had signed. The route now takes express.raw before any parser exists,
verifies the buffer, and parses those same bytes.

AND THE CONFIRMATION LINK WAS A CONVENTION, NOT A CONSTRAINT. tasks.confirmation_id had
no foreign key, so an orphan task inserted cleanly, and confirmations.member_id had none
either, so a service account could "confirm". There are now composite foreign keys on
both, plus draft and message, and the database rejects a dangling id even if every line
of application code is wrong.

Alongside those: the confirmation and its task are written in one transaction; a
provider outage is metered as an outage instead of as a good day; the worker survives a
failing job instead of exiting; rate limits on the webhook, login, confirm and demo;
request size limits; an error handler that returns a page instead of a stack trace.

Retention arrived too, in src/retention.ts: message bodies are nulled in place while
hashes and identifiers survive so dedupe still works, disconnecting a source expires
pending drafts rather than silently confirming them, deleting a workspace leaves the
audit trail standing, and every sweep writes counts and never content. It also caught
that body was NOT NULL, which made "null the content" impossible; the column is nullable
now and the worker skips a message whose content has already gone.

49 tests, all offline behind the deterministic fake, typecheck clean.
